<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Chimalpopoca</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. <a href="/years/1417">1417</a>–<a href="/years/1427">1427</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Chimalpopoca was the third ruler of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexica">Mexica</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tenochtitlan">Tenochtitlan</a> in the early fifteenth century, when they were tributaries of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tepanecs">Tepanec</a> city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azcapotzalco">Azcapotzalco</a>. During the succession struggle after the death of the Tepanec ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tezozomoc">Tezozomoc</a> in 1426, he was allegedly murdered on the orders of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maxtla">Maxtla</a>. His successor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/itzcoatl">Itzcoatl</a> formed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aztec-empire">Triple Alliance</a> that founded the Aztec Empire.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/chimalpopoca.jpg" alt="Chimalpopoca" width="320" height="246" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Tovar, Juan de, circa 1546-circa 1626 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chimalpopoca,_the_Third_Aztec_King_(Reigned_1417%E2%80%9327)_WDL6720.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Chimalpopoca, whose name means smoking shield in Classical Nahuatl, was born in 1397, the son of the ruler Huitzilihuitl and Queen Ayauhcihuatl. Some sources instead make him a son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acamapichtli">Acamapichtli</a>, the first ruler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tenochtitlan">Tenochtitlan</a>, and so a brother of Huitzilihuitl, and the friar Gerónimo de Mendieta, noting the discrepancy, concluded that Huitzilihuitl, Chimalpopoca and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/itzcoatl">Itzcoatl</a> must have been brothers. He was a grandson of both Acamapichtli and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tezozomoc">Tezozomoc</a>, ruler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azcapotzalco">Azcapotzalco</a>, and a half-brother of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moctezuma-i">Moctezuma I</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>He was crowned at about 20 years old, in 1417 by most accounts, though some sources give 1416 or 1418. The bond with his grandfather was strengthened by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tenochtitlan">Tenochtitlan</a>'s loyalty in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tezozomoc">Tezozomoc</a>'s war of 1418 against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ixtlilxochitl-i">Ixtlilxochitl I</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/texcoco">Texcoco</a>, after which the conquered city was granted to Tenochtitlan as a tributary. Chimalpopoca interceded with Tezozomoc for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nezahualcoyotl">Nezahualcoyotl</a>, the displaced prince of Texcoco, who was then allowed to live in Tenochtitlan under his protection.</p><p>With <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tezozomoc">Tezozomoc</a>'s help he built a wooden aqueduct from the high ground of Chapultepec to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tenochtitlan">Tenochtitlan</a> in 1426, and he had a causeway built to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tlacopan">Tlacopan</a> with openings spanned by wooden bridges that were taken up at night. He dedicated a sacrificial stone in the Tlacocomoco quarter, and the conquest of Tequizquiac is attributed to him. He had many wives, among them his cousin Matlalatzin, and his son Tezozomoc became king of Ecatepec.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tezozomoc">Tezozomoc</a> died in 1426 his son Tayauh succeeded him, but another son, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maxtla">Maxtla</a>, ruler of Coyoacan, seized the throne. Chimalpopoca had sided with Tayauh, and Maxtla had him killed, though the details are unclear, and raised the tribute demanded of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tenochtitlan">Tenochtitlan</a>. Itzcoatl, who followed him as tlatoani, allied with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nezahualcoyotl">Nezahualcoyotl</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/texcoco">Texcoco</a> and Totoquihuatzin of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tlacopan">Tlacopan</a>, and together they brought down Maxtla.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Chimalpopoca” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The death of the long-reigning Tepanec ruler Tezozomoc in 1426 set off a succession struggle during which his son Maxtla reportedly had the Mexica ruler <b>Chimalpopoca</b> murdered.</blockquote><a href="/empire/aztec-empire">Aztec Empire · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tenochtitlan">Tenochtitlan</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/mexico">Mexico</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tenochtitlan">Tenochtitlan</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/mexico">Mexico</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=19.44,-99.13&amp;year=1422" aria-label="Tenochtitlan on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="1872 1444 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Chimalpopoca"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M2322 1714h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M2322 1714h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=19.44,-99.13&amp;year=1422">What was here in 1422? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
